layout "layout.faqir"

block content
  p
    | Claude Code stops and waits whenever it needs you: to approve a command, to answer a question, to approve a plan,
    | or simply because it finished and wants the next instruction. At your desk that is fine. Away from it, Claude sits
    | idle until you come back.
    strong Walk mode
    | moves those moments to your phone.

  h2#on-off What changes when it is on
  div(data-ui="grid" data-cols="1" data-cols-md="2" data-gap="4")
    +card(title="Walk mode off (the default)")
      @body
        ul
          li The plugin only reports what happens.
          li Permission prompts, questions and plans appear in the terminal as usual.
          li Nothing ever waits on the server, so your sessions are never slowed down.
    +card(title="Walk mode on")
      @body
        ul
          li Permission prompts, questions and plans become Inbox cards and notifications, and Claude waits for your answer.
          li At the end of each turn Claude waits up to 5 minutes for your next instruction ("Turn finished").
          li Your answer goes straight back to Claude, which carries on.

  h2#turn-on Turning it on and off
  p There are three places, from broadest to narrowest:
  dl
    dt
      strong For everything:
      | the
      strong Walk mode
      | switch in the
      a(href="/docs/inbox") Inbox
    dd The global setting. Every session follows it unless its machine or the session itself says otherwise.
    dt
      strong For one machine:
      | the
      strong Walk mode
      | control on its card in
      a(href="/docs/machines") Machines
    dd
      strong Inherit
      | follows the global setting,
      strong On
      | means "always on for this machine" and
      strong Off
      | means "never waits for you here". Handy for a machine that runs unattended jobs.
    dt
      strong For one session:
      | the
      strong Walk mode
      | switch in its
      a(href="/docs/sessions#controls") header
      | , or the
      code /bb-walk
      | command inside Claude Code
    dd The narrowest setting, and the one that wins.
  h3#which-wins Which setting wins
  p
    | Buildyboy looks at the session first: if the session has its own setting, that is used. If not, it uses the
    | machine's setting, unless the machine is on
    strong Inherit
    | , in which case the global switch decides.
  +callout(variant="warning")
    @content
      p
        | Once you have switched walk mode on or off for a session, that session keeps its own setting. Turning the global
        | switch off later does
        strong not
        | turn it off for that session. Switch it off on the session itself, or type
        code /bb-walk off
        | in it.

  h2#bb-walk The /bb-walk command
  p Inside any Claude Code session on a paired machine, type:
  dl
    dt
      code /bb-walk
      | or
      code /bb-walk on
    dd Walk mode on for this session.
    dt
      code /bb-walk off
    dd Walk mode off for this session.
    dt
      code /bb-walk 5m
    dd
      | Walk mode on, and at the end of each turn wait up to 5 minutes for your reply. Any number from 0 to 9 works (for
      | example
      code 2.5m
      | );
      code 0m
      | means "do not wait at the end of a turn at all".
  p
    | It replies with a line such as "Walk mode ON for this session; at the end of a turn Claude waits up to 5 min for
    | your reply." If the server does not know the session yet, it applies to the whole machine instead and says so.
  +callout(variant="muted")
    @content
      p
        | Anything other than
        code off
        | turns walk mode on, so a typo such as
        code /bb-walk of
        | switches it on. Check the reply.

  h2#waiting How long things wait
  div(data-ui="table")
    table(data-part="table")
      thead(data-part="thead")
        tr(data-part="tr")
          th(data-part="th" scope="col") What
          th(data-part="th" scope="col") How long
          th(data-part="th" scope="col") If you do not answer
      tbody(data-part="tbody")
        tr(data-part="tr")
          td(data-part="td") Permission, question, plan
          td(data-part="td") about 9½ minutes
          td(data-part="td") Claude Code's normal behaviour takes over: in a terminal the usual prompt appears; in an unattended claude -p run the request is denied.
        tr(data-part="tr")
          td(data-part="td") Turn finished
          td(data-part="td") 5 minutes by default, at most 9
          td(data-part="td") The session goes idle, as it would have anyway.
        tr(data-part="tr")
          td(data-part="td") Pause (per session or Pause everything)
          td(data-part="td") about 9½ minutes per tool call
          td(data-part="td") The held tool runs, and the next tool call is held again.
  p
    | The end-of-turn wait can be changed in three ways:
    code /bb-walk Nm
    | for one session,
    strong Hold at Stop (minutes)
    | in
    a(href="/docs/settings#budgets") Settings
    | for everyone, or the server's
    code BUILDYBOY_STOP_WAIT_MIN
    | setting. The session's own value wins, then Settings, then the server default. Values above 9 minutes are treated
    | as 9, because Claude Code gives up on a hook after 10.

  h2#answering-late Answering late, or at the desk
  ul
    li
      strong You answered at the terminal first.
      | The card moves to History as "Answered at the terminal" and leaves the Inbox.
    li
      strong The wait ran out before you answered.
      | Answering now shows "Already answered (probably at your desk)." Nothing is lost: if Claude is at its prompt,
      | send your instruction from the session page instead; it is delivered at the next turn.
    li
      strong The server restarted while something was waiting.
      | The waiting request is released and recorded as timed out.

  h2#server-down If the server cannot be reached
  p
    | Walk mode relies on the server. If the hook cannot reach it, it does what it always does: it gives up within a
    | second and lets Claude Code behave normally. Prompts appear at the terminal and your phone sees nothing until the
    | connection is back. Events are kept in the local spool and sent later.

  h2#good-habits Good habits
  ol
    li
      | Before leaving, check notifications work with
      strong Send test notification
      | in
      a(href="/docs/notifications") Settings → Notifications
      | .
    li
      | Turn walk mode on in the Inbox (or with
      code /bb-walk 5m
      | in the session you care about).
    li
      | Keep an eye on the attention bar; "1 waiting for you" is your cue.
    li
      strong Turn walk mode off when you are back.
      | Otherwise every finished turn at your desk waits up to 5 minutes for a reply that you would rather type in the
      | terminal.
